= Homeostasis =

== Overview ==
'''Homeostasis''' is the maintenance of relatively stable internal conditions in a biological system despite changing external conditions and internal demands. It involves regulatory processes that keep certain variables within viable ranges; it does not require that every variable remain fixed or that the organism be at thermodynamic equilibrium.

== Regulation and feedback ==
A regulated variable such as body temperature or plasma glucose concentration is affected by sensors, signaling pathways and effectors. [[Related to::Feedback|Negative feedback]] can counter a disturbance by altering the processes that influence the regulated variable. Not every negative-feedback loop is homeostatic, and not every regulatory process has a single fixed set point. Predictive or anticipatory responses can also contribute.

== Cell and organism scales ==
At cellular scale, selective transport and metabolic processes help regulate ion concentrations, pH and osmotic conditions. [[Related to::Cell membrane|Cell membranes]] permit the maintenance of gradients while specialized transporters and other cellular mechanisms sustain them. At organism scale, multiple tissues and endocrine, neural or local regulatory systems cooperate to maintain conditions compatible with function.

== Examples ==
During physical activity, production of heat increases. Changes in blood flow, sweating and other responses can help regulate temperature. Blood glucose regulation involves interacting hormonal, metabolic and behavioral factors. Such mechanisms have limits and may respond differently across species or physiological states.

== Homeostasis versus equilibrium and allostasis ==
A living cell is an open, energy-consuming system. Its internal concentrations may be approximately steady while there are ongoing flows of matter and energy. Thus homeostasis is not the same as [[Chemical equilibrium|chemical equilibrium]]. The term '''allostasis''' emphasizes stability through adaptive changes, including altered operating points and anticipatory adjustments; the two concepts should be distinguished by mechanism and modeling context.

== Evidence and limits ==
Claiming a variable is homeostatically controlled requires identifying the relevant conditions, regulation, observations and plausible mechanism. A single constant measurement does not establish a control loop; a feedback diagram alone does not prove homeostasis.

== Energy-dependent maintenance ==
[[Related to::Metabolism|Metabolism]] supplies energy and materials for many homeostatic processes. Membrane pumps, biosynthesis, transport and repair can maintain approximately steady intracellular conditions even while molecules continually enter, leave or react. Thus a homeostatic state is not a claim that a cell has reached thermodynamic equilibrium or that all reaction rates have fallen to zero.

== Linking measurements to mechanism ==
A stable observation needs a specified variable, time interval and measurement precision. To establish active regulation, investigators may perturb the system and observe an appropriate compensatory response. A correlation between two physiological variables may suggest a relationship but does not by itself identify the feedback pathway or its controlling mechanism.

